{{!
	PinnableElement/Open: the opening half of the wrapper every pinnable region of this skin is
	built from. A pinnable region is a menu-like block the reader can dock into a layout column
	(pinned) or tuck away inside a dropdown (unpinned). Exactly four regions use this pair and no
	others: the appearance panel, the main menu, the page tools menu and the table of contents.

	Data dictionary supplied by NotionComponentPinnableElement::getTemplateData(), which returns
	exactly one entry. Any other key visible here arrives from a caller that unioned another
	component's data into the same context, and is deliberately not read by this partial:
	string id the region's identifier, already carrying the `notion-` prefix because each of the
		four callers injects its own self::ID. The only values are `notion-appearance`,
		`notion-main-menu`, `notion-page-tools` and `notion-toc`. It is emitted TWICE below, once
		as the HTML id attribute and again as the FIRST class, and both halves are load-bearing:
		- the class is the hook the per-region stylesheets target -- .notion-appearance,
		  .notion-main-menu, .notion-page-tools and .notion-toc -- because a class keeps those
		  selectors low in specificity and therefore still overridable by gadgets and extensions,
		  while the literal second class below is the hook for the rules shared by all four;
		- the id is what resources/skins.notion.js resolves with document.getElementById in order
		  to relocate the region between its two containers, and it is also the stem of the
		  region's own `-unpinned-popup` message key.
		Dropping either half breaks styling or pinning SILENTLY, raising nothing anywhere, so the
		duplication is deliberate and must survive any later tidying. The value is emitted
		verbatim for the same reason: NotionComponentPinnableHeader appends `-pinned-container`
		and `-unpinned-container` to this very string, so decorating or transforming it here
		would desynchronise a region from its own mount points.
	object data-pinnable-header OPTIONAL. The NotionComponentPinnableHeader payload. When present,
		that partial renders the region's label and its pin and unpin buttons inside this wrapper;
		when absent the region simply has no header row and everything else still works. That
		partial alone owns the pinned and unpinned state class and the four data-* attributes the
		pinning script reads, so none of them is repeated here.

	The structure below is a runtime contract rather than a preference. Exactly ONE element is
	opened, and the header is nested within it:
	- the pinning script compares the region's parentElement id against the two container ids
	  before moving it, so the region has to be a DIRECT child of its container; an intervening
	  wrapper would leave that comparison unable to match either id;
	- after a toggle the same script looks for the unpin button WITHIN the region, so the header
	  has to be a descendant of this element rather than a sibling of it.
	Both containers are emitted by the PinnableContainer partials on every render, so the region
	is server-rendered into the correct one and stays usable and correctly styled when JavaScript
	never runs; the script only moves it afterwards.

	This wrapper carries no ARIA and no presentational attribute by design. The surrounding <nav>
	landmark supplies the accessible region and the header's buttons supply their own labels, so
	repeating either here would announce the region twice or contradict the landmark. Appearance
	arrives entirely from resources/skins.notion.styles, which is why there is no inline style and
	no literal value of any kind to keep out of this file.
}}
{{! this template must be used with PinnableElement/Close to avoid unclosed HTML tags }}
<div id="{{id}}" class="{{id}} notion-pinnable-element">
	{{#data-pinnable-header}}{{>PinnableHeader}}{{/data-pinnable-header}}
